---
import SiteLayout from "../../../../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../../../../../../components/Eyebrow.astro";
---
<SiteLayout title="Module 5: Number Systems — fred.fish" description="Source-based lessons for binary, decimal, and hexadecimal number systems used in networking." active="cisco">
  <section class="source-lesson">
    <div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
    <header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 05" label="MODULE 5 / NUMBER SYSTEMS" /><h1>Read the numbers.<br /><span>Work with the bits.</span></h1><p>Networking uses several number systems. Binary represents the bits computers process, decimal is familiar to people, and hexadecimal provides a compact way to write binary values.</p></header>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Understand positional number systems</h2><p>A positional number system assigns each digit a value based on its position and the system's base. Decimal is base 10 and uses digits 0–9. Binary is base 2 and uses 0 and 1. Hexadecimal is base 16 and uses 0–9 plus A–F, where A=10, B=11, C=12, D=13, E=14, and F=15. The rightmost position represents the lowest power of the base.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0390 · IMG-0393</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Convert binary to decimal</h2><p>Each bit position in an 8-bit octet has a power-of-two weight: 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, and 1. Add the weights where the bit is 1. For example, 11000000₂ is 128 + 64 = 192₁₀. The binary value 11111111₂ has every bit set, so its decimal value is 255.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0395</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Convert decimal to binary</h2><p>To convert a decimal value to binary, determine which powers of two sum to the value. For 192, use 128 and 64, so the first two bits are 1 and the remaining six bits are 0: 11000000. For an IPv4 octet, the result must fit in eight bits, representing values from 0 through 255.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0395 · IMG-0398</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">04</div><div><h2>Convert hexadecimal to binary and decimal</h2><p>One hexadecimal digit represents exactly four bits, called a nibble. For example, hexadecimal C is decimal 12 and binary 1100. Hexadecimal FF is 15 × 16 + 15 = 255, or binary 11111111. Two hexadecimal digits therefore represent one 8-bit octet. This makes hexadecimal useful for compactly writing MAC addresses and IPv6 addresses.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0393 · IMG-0400</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">05</div><div><h2>Why networking uses these systems</h2><p>IPv4 addresses and subnet masks are commonly displayed in decimal dotted notation, but subnet calculations depend on binary network and host bits. MAC addresses and IPv6 addresses are commonly written in hexadecimal because their binary representations are long. Being able to convert between systems makes it easier to identify network boundaries, prefix lengths, and address values.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE TOPICS</span><code>IPv4 addressing · IPv6 addressing · Ethernet MAC addresses</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="worked-example"><Eyebrow label="WORKED EXAMPLE" /><h2>Convert 192 to binary</h2><div class="weights"><span>128</span><span>64</span><span>32</span><span>16</span><span>8</span><span>4</span><span>2</span><span>1</span></div><div class="bits"><b>1</b><b>1</b><b>0</b><b>0</b><b>0</b><b>0</b><b>0</b><b>0</b></div><p>128 + 64 = 192, so <code>11000000₂ = 192₁₀</code>.</p></section>
    <section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Practice conversions.</h2><p>Build speed with binary place values, hexadecimal nibbles, and networking examples.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
